```java
// Hand-rolled checks clutter the controller and miss cases
@PostMapping("/users")
ResponseEntity<String> register(@RequestBody UserRequest req) {
    if (req.username() == null || req.username().isBlank())
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("username required");
    if (req.email() == null || !req.email().contains("@"))
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("bad email");
    if (req.age() == null || req.age() < 18)
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("must be 18+");
    // business logic finally starts here...
    return ResponseEntity.ok("registered");
}
```

```java
// Declare constraints once on the DTO (Spring Boot 3.2, Java 17)
public record UserRequest(
    @NotBlank(message = "Username is required")
    @Size(min = 3, max = 20, message = "Username must be 3-20 characters")
    String username,

    @NotBlank @Email(message = "Invalid email format")
    String email,

    @NotNull @Min(value = 18, message = "Must be at least 18")
    Integer age
) {}

@PostMapping("/users")
ResponseEntity<String> register(@Valid @RequestBody UserRequest req) { // @Valid triggers it
    return ResponseEntity.ok("registered: " + req.username());          // runs only if valid
}
```

A single advice formats failures cleanly:

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
class ValidationAdvice {
    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    ResponseEntity<Map<String, String>> onInvalid(MethodArgumentNotValidException e) {
        var errors = e.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(FieldError::getField, FieldError::getDefaultMessage));
        return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(errors);
    }
}
```

```java
// starter-web: return the object directly; the thread blocks on the repo call
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private final OrderRepository repo;
    OrderController(OrderRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }

    @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
    Order get(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return repo.findById(id).orElseThrow(); // blocking JDBC/JPA call
    }
}
```

```java
// starter-webflux: return Mono/Flux; nothing blocks, but the WHOLE stack must be reactive
@RestController
class OrderController {
    private final ReactiveOrderRepository repo; // R2DBC, not JPA
    OrderController(ReactiveOrderRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }

    @GetMapping("/orders/{id}")
    Mono<Order> get(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return repo.findById(id);   // returns a publisher, no blocking
    }
}
```

Key trap: using WebFlux with a blocking JDBC driver makes the event-loop threads block — worse than MVC. WebFlux only pays off with reactive drivers like R2DBC.
